================================================================================
POCKET-CMS
================================================================================

DESCRIPTION
-----------
Pocket-CMS is a web content management system made up of less than 200 lines of
Perl code in a CGI script (plus a couple of libraries). Web pages are the output
of Perl functions provided by content files. It uses no sessions and visitors
are given no cookies by default. It provides no special means for logging or
analyzing traffic and doesn't aid with SEO.

Pocket-CMS comes with a blogging module (162 lines of code) with no feedback
functionality and no built-in way to analyze traffic. It does paginate and
create an index, though. It also serves as an example of a dynamic web page.

Example site: https://henningstrandin.com

FEATURES
--------
- File-based content storage.
- Dynamic menu generation from file names.
- Simple subpage and submenu support.
- Content is dynamic by default.
- Uses Text::Xslate for page templates.
- Styling with CSS.
- Pages can be visible or hidden.
- No built-in analytics.
- No built-in SEO support.
- A healthy "don't care" attitude.

REQUIREMENTS
------------
- Perl 5.x
- Perl modules:
  - CGI::Tiny
  - Text::Xslate
  - File::Slurp

INSTALLATION
------------
1. Clone or download this repository.
2. Ensure Perl and the required modules are installed.
3. Ensure your web server executes *.cgi files as CGI scripts in the directory
   where you place the Pocket-CMS files.
4. Move the Pocket-CMS files there.
5. Make index.cgi and next-ID.pl executable.

DIRECTORY STRUCTURE
-------------------
.
├── cnt
│   ├── 0--0000--T--050--Example   # Example first page.
│   ├── 0--0001--T--100--The_Blog  # The blog module.
│   └── blog
│       └── 0--en--2000-01-01--Example_blog_entry.html
├── images
│   ├── globe_clock.jpg
│   └── Pocket-CMS-logo.jpg
├── index.cgi
├── index.tmpl
├── main_style.css
├── new_page_template.txt
├── next-ID.pl  # Run this before creating a new page.
└── README

CONTENT FILES
-------------

* FILE NAMES

Content files in the "cnt/" directory follow this naming convention:
[L]--[ID]--[PAR-ID]--[order]--[name]

L:      0 if visible, 1 if hidden behind "login."
ID:     Unique ID for page.
PAR-ID: ID of parent page if a subpage, "T" otherwise.
ORDER:  Place in menu ordering.
NAME:   Name of the page displayed in menu.

* CREATING A NEW WEB PAGE

Run ./next-ID.pl to get next free page ID and current top-level menu ordering.

Use new_page_template.txt to start creating a new page.

Each content file defines a Perl subroutine named "page_content". The
page_content subroutine receives the following when called by the main script:

- Request parameters and cookies
- Name of calling script
- The ID of this page
- The ID of the requested sub-page of this page
- User "logged in" status
- Path to main content folder

The page_content subroutine should return the following data structure:

$pnc{'p_cont'}{'title'}                       Title shown in window title bar.
$pnc{'p_cont'}{'head'}                        Main page header.
$pnc{'p_cont'}{'style'}                       CSS code in HTML header.
$pnc{'p_cont'}{'javascript'}                  Javascript in HTML header.
$pnc{'p_cont'}{'js_src'}                      Javascript source file to be
                                              loaded.
$pnc{'p_cont'}{'center_content'}              Page body HTML content.

$pnc{'submenu'}[[ORDER]]{'name'}              Name of submenu entry.
$pnc{'submenu'}[[ORDER]]{'ID'}                ID of subpage. (Local to this
                                              parent page.)
$pnc{'submenu'}[[ORDER]]{'locked'}            Set to 1 if this page is hidden
                                              behind "login." [ORDER] is a
                                              positive integer menu position.
$pnc{'current_sub'}                           ID of current subpage.

$pnc{'cookies'}{'[COOKIE_NAME]'}{'value'}     Value of the cookie COOKIE_NAME to
                                              be set.
$pnc{'cookies'}{'[COOKIE_NAME]'}{'expires'}   Expiration time of cookie
                                              COOKIE_NAME to set.

AUTHENTICATION -- NO SUCH THING!
--------------------------------
Pages can be hidden from regular visitors and displayed only when the "password"
is entered. This is meant as a staging area and for the convenience of the
visitors, it's not secure in any way what so ever. Control the visibility of the
page with the leading 0 or 1 of the file name.

TEMPLATING
----------
Pocket-CMS uses Text::Xslate to render pages using the "index.tmpl" template.
See the provided "index.tmpl" for names of placeholders.

USAGE
-----
1. Run ./next-ID.pl to get next free page ID and current top-level menu
   ordering, and be reminded of content file naming rules.
2. Give a copy of new_page_template.txt the name of the new content file.
   Start file name with "1" if you want to hide this page until it's ready.
3. Put the new content file under cnt/.
4. Create the content. (Emacs is a great content creation UI for Pocket-CMS.)
5. Test and review your new page.
6. Make the page visible by changing the file name, if required.

EXAMPLE CONTENT FILE
--------------------
sub page_content {
    my %pnc;
    
    $pnc{'p_cont'}{'title'} = q{Pocket-CMS Example Page};
    $pnc{'p_cont'}{'head'} = q{Pocket-CMS Example Page};

    $pnc{'p_cont'}{'center_content'} = q{
        <p>This is an example web page delivered by Pocket-CMS.</p>

        <div style="float: right; padding: 10px;">
            <img src="images/Pocket-CMS-logo.jpg" alt="Pocket-CMS logo"
                style="width: 200px; border-radius: 8px;">
        </div>

        <p>Pocket-CMS was quaintly hand-coded without AI-assistance.</p>

        <p>Pocket-CMS comes with a blogging module!</p>
    };
    
    return (\%pnc);
}
1;

